</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>(a) Linear interpolation of data input <math id="S2.F2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{0},X_{1})\sim{\pi}_{0}\times{\pi}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{0},X_{1})\sim{\pi}_{0}\times{\pi}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
(b) The rectified flow <math id="S2.F2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> induced by <math id="S2.F2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{0},X_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{0},X_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>;
the trajectories are “rewired” at the intersection points
to avoid the crossing.
(c) The linear interpolation of the end points <math id="S2.F2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Z_{0},Z_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Z_{0},Z_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> of flow <math id="S2.F2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
(d) The rectified flow induced from <math id="S2.F2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Z_{0},Z_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Z_{0},Z_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>, which follows straight paths.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Overview</h3>

<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Rectified flow</h5>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given empirical observations of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}\sim{\pi}_{0},X_{1}\sim{\pi}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}\sim{\pi}_{0},X_{1}\sim{\pi}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>,
the rectified flow induced from <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{0},X_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{0},X_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>
is an ordinary differentiable model (ODE)
on time <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{d}Z_{t}=v(Z_{t},t)\mathrm{d}t," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{d}Z_{t}=v(Z_{t},t)\mathrm{d}t,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which converts <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to a <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> following <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The drift force
<math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\colon\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\colon\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is set to drive the flow to
follow the direction <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{1}-X_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{1}-X_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>
of the linear path pointing from <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>
as much as possible, by solving a simple
least squares regression problem:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{v}\int_{0}^{1}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\lVert(X_{1}-X_{0})-v\big(X_{t},\penalty\ t\big)\right\rVert^{2}\right]\mathrm{d}t,\penalty\ \penalty\ \penalty\ \penalty\ \penalty\ \text{with}\penalty\ \penalty\ \penalty\ \penalty\ X_{t}=tX_{1}+(1-t)X_{0}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mmultiscripts><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>v</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mstyle><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.667em">,</mo><mtext>with</mtext><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{v}\int_{0}^{1}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\lVert(X_{1}-X_{0})-v\big(X_{t},\penalty\ t\big)\right\rVert^{2}\right]\mathrm{d}t,\penalty\ \penalty\ \penalty\ \penalty\ \penalty\ \text{with}\penalty\ \penalty\ \penalty\ \penalty\ X_{t}=tX_{1}+(1-t)X_{0},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is
the linear interpolation of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Naviely, <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> follows the ODE of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{d}X_{t}=(X_{1}-X_{0})\mathrm{d}t," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{d}X_{t}=(X_{1}-X_{0})\mathrm{d}t,</annotation></semantics></math> which is non-causal (or anticipating) as the update of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> requires the information of the final point <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
By fitting the drift <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}-X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}-X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>,
the rectified flow <em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">causalizes</em>
the paths of linear interpolation <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>,
yielding an ODE flow that can be simulated without seeing the future.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In practice, we parameterize <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> with a neural network or other nonlinear models
and solve
(<a href="#S2.E1" title="In Rectified flow ‣ 2.1 Overview ‣ 2 Method ‣ Flow Straight and Fast: Learning to Generate and Transfer Data with Rectified Flow" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) with any off-the-shelf stochastic optimizer, such as stochastic gradient descent,
with empirical draws of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{0},X_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{0},X_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>. See
Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ Straight line flows yield fast simulation ‣ 2.1 Overview ‣ 2 Method ‣ Flow Straight and Fast: Learning to Generate and Transfer Data with Rectified Flow" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
After we get <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, we solve the ODE starting from <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{0}\sim{\pi}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{0}\sim{\pi}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to transfer <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>,
backwardly starting from <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{1}\sim{\pi}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{1}\sim{\pi}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> to transfer <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically,
for backward sampling,
we simply
solve <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{d}\tilde{X}_{t}=-v(\tilde{X}_{t},t)\mathrm{d}t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{d}\tilde{X}_{t}=-v(\tilde{X}_{t},t)\mathrm{d}t</annotation></semantics></math> initialized from <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{X}_{0}\sim{\pi}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{X}_{0}\sim{\pi}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and set
<math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}=\tilde{X}_{1-t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}=\tilde{X}_{1-t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The forward and backward sampling
are equally favored by the training algorithm, because the objective in (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In Rectified flow ‣ 2.1 Overview ‣ 2 Method ‣ Flow Straight and Fast: Learning to Generate and Transfer Data with Rectified Flow" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) is
<em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">time-symmetric</em>
in that it yields the equivalent problem if we exchange <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and flip the sign of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Flows avoid crossing</h5>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A key to understanding the method is the non-crossing property of flows:
the different paths
following a well defined ODE <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{d}Z_{t}=v(Z_{t},t)\mathrm{d}t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{d}Z_{t}=v(Z_{t},t)\mathrm{d}t</annotation></semantics></math>,
whose solution exists and is unique, <em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cannot cross each other</em> at any time <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Specifically,
there exists no location <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and time <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, such that two paths go across <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> at time <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> along different directions, because otherwise the solution of the ODE would be non-unique.
On the other hand, the paths of the interpolation process <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> may intersect with each other (Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2 Method ‣ Flow Straight and Fast: Learning to Generate and Transfer Data with Rectified Flow" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>a), which makes it non-causal.
Hence, as shown in Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2 Method ‣ Flow Straight and Fast: Learning to Generate and Transfer Data with Rectified Flow" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>b,
the rectified flow
<em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">rewires</em> the individual trajectories passing through the intersection points to avoid crossing, while
tracing out the same density map as the linear interpolation paths
due to the optimization of
(<a href="#S2.E1" title="In Rectified flow ‣ 2.1 Overview ‣ 2 Method ‣ Flow Straight and Fast: Learning to Generate and Transfer Data with Rectified Flow" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).
We can view the linear interpolation <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> as building
roads (or tunnels) to connect <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and the rectified flow as traffics of particles passing through the roads in a myopic, memoryless, non-crossing way,
which allows them to ignore the global
path information of how <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> are paired,
and rebuild a more deterministic pairing of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Z_{0},Z_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Z_{0},Z_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Rectified flows reduce transport costs</h5>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In Rectified flow ‣ 2.1 Overview ‣ 2 Method ‣ Flow Straight and Fast: Learning to Generate and Transfer Data with Rectified Flow" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) is solved exactly,
the pair <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Z_{0},Z_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Z_{0},Z_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>
of the rectified flow
is guaranteed to be a valid coupling of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi}_{0},{\pi}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi}_{0},{\pi}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> (Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 3.3. ‣ 3.1 The Marginal Preserving Property ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Flow Straight and Fast: Learning to Generate and Transfer Data with Rectified Flow" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>),
that is, <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> follows <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{0}\sim{\pi}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{0}\sim{\pi}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Moreover, <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Z_{0},Z_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Z_{0},Z_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> guarantees to yield no larger transport cost than the data pair <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{0},X_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{0},X_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> simultaneously for <em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">all</em> convex cost functions <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> (Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 3.5. ‣ 3.2 Reducing Convex Transport Costs ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Flow Straight and Fast: Learning to Generate and Transfer Data with Rectified Flow" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>).
The data pair <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{0},X_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{0},X_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> can be an arbitrary coupling of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi}_{0},{\pi}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi}_{0},{\pi}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, typically independent (i.e., <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{0},X_{1})\sim{\pi}_{0}\times{\pi}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{0},X_{1})\sim{\pi}_{0}\times{\pi}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>)
as dictated by the lack of meaningfully paired observations in practical problems. In comparison,
the rectified coupling <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Z_{0},Z_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Z_{0},Z_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> has a deterministic dependency as it is constructed from an ODE model.
Denote by <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Z_{0},Z_{1})=\mathtt{Rectify}((X_{0},X_{1}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝚁𝚎𝚌𝚝𝚒𝚏𝚢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Z_{0},Z_{1})=\mathtt{Rectify}((X_{0},X_{1}))</annotation></semantics></math>
the mapping from <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{0},X_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{0},X_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Z_{0},Z_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Z_{0},Z_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence, <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{Rectify}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚁𝚎𝚌𝚝𝚒𝚏𝚢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{Rectify}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> converts an arbitrary coupling into a deterministic coupling with lower convex transport costs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Straight line flows yield fast simulation</h5>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ Straight line flows yield fast simulation ‣ 2.1 Overview ‣ 2 Method ‣ Flow Straight and Fast: Learning to Generate and Transfer Data with Rectified Flow" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>,
denote by <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\boldsymbol{Z}}=\mathtt{RectFlow}((X_{0},X_{1}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒁</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝚁𝚎𝚌𝚝𝙵𝚕𝚘𝚠</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\boldsymbol{Z}}=\mathtt{RectFlow}((X_{0},X_{1}))</annotation></semantics></math> the rectified flow induced from <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{0},X_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{0},X_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Applying this operator recursively
yields a sequence of rectified flows
<math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\boldsymbol{Z}}^{k+1}=\mathtt{RectFlow}((Z_{0}^{k},Z_{1}^{k}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝒁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝚁𝚎𝚌𝚝𝙵𝚕𝚘𝚠</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\boldsymbol{Z}}^{k+1}=\mathtt{RectFlow}((Z_{0}^{k},Z_{1}^{k}))</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Z_{0}^{0},Z_{1}^{0})=(X_{0},X_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Z_{0}^{0},Z_{1}^{0})=(X_{0},X_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\boldsymbol{Z}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒁</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\boldsymbol{Z}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th rectified flow, or simply <em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-rectified flow</em>, induced from <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{0},X_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{0},X_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This
<em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">reflow</em>
procedure not only decreases transport cost,
but also has the important effect of
straightening paths of rectified flows,
that is, making the paths of the flow more straight.
This is highly attractive computationally
as flows with nearly straight paths
incur small time-discretization error
in numerical simulation. Indeed, perfectly straight paths can be simulated exactly with a single Euler step and is effectively a one-step model.
This addresses the very bottleneck of high inference cost
in existing continuous-time ODE/SDE models.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Rectified Flow: Main Algorithm</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
                
                
               <span id="alg1.l1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Procedure</span>: <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\boldsymbol{Z}}={\mathtt{RectFlow}((X_{0},X_{1}))}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒁</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝚁𝚎𝚌𝚝𝙵𝚕𝚘𝚠</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\boldsymbol{Z}}={\mathtt{RectFlow}((X_{0},X_{1}))}</annotation></semantics></math>:

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
                
                
                  <em id="alg1.l2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Inputs</em>: Draws from a coupling <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{0},X_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{0},X_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="alg1.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="alg1.l2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>; velocity model <math id="alg1.l2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\theta}\colon\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\theta}\colon\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> with parameter <math id="alg1.l2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta.</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
                
                
                 <em id="alg1.l3.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Training</em>:
<math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{\theta}=\argmin_{\theta}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\lVert X_{1}-X_{0}-v(tX_{1}+(1-t)X_{0},\penalty\ t)\right\rVert^{2}\right]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mi>𝔼</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>tX</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{\theta}=\argmin_{\theta}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\lVert X_{1}-X_{0}-v(tX_{1}+(1-t)X_{0},\penalty\ t)\right\rVert^{2}\right],</annotation></semantics></math>
with <math id="alg1.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\sim\textrm{Uniform}([0,1])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mtext>Uniform</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\sim\textrm{Uniform}([0,1])</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
                
                
                 <em id="alg1.l4.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Sampling</em>: Draw <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Z_{0},Z_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Z_{0},Z_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> following <math id="alg1.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{d}Z_{t}=v_{\hat{\theta}}(Z_{t},t)\mathrm{d}t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{d}Z_{t}=v_{\hat{\theta}}(Z_{t},t)\mathrm{d}t</annotation></semantics></math> starting from <math id="alg1.l4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{0}\sim{\pi}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{0}\sim{\pi}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> (or backwardly <math id="alg1.l4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{1}\sim{\pi}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{1}\sim{\pi}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>).

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
                
                
                 <em id="alg1.l5.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Return</em>: <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\boldsymbol{Z}}=\{Z_{t}\colon t\in[0,1]\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒁</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\boldsymbol{Z}}=\{Z_{t}\colon t\in[0,1]\}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
                
                
               <span id="alg1.l6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reflow</span> (optional): <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\boldsymbol{Z}}^{k+1}=\mathtt{RectFlow}((Z_{0}^{k},Z_{1}^{k}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝒁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝚁𝚎𝚌𝚝𝙵𝚕𝚘𝚠</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\boldsymbol{Z}}^{k+1}=\mathtt{RectFlow}((Z_{0}^{k},Z_{1}^{k}))</annotation></semantics></math>, starting from <math id="alg1.l6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Z^{0}_{0},Z^{0}_{1})=(X_{0},X_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Z^{0}_{0},Z^{0}_{1})=(X_{0},X_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
                
                
               <span id="alg1.l7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Distill</span> (optional): Learn a neural network <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{T}</annotation></semantics></math> to distill the <math id="alg1.l7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-rectified flow, such that <math id="alg1.l7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{k}_{1}\approx\hat{T}(Z^{k}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{k}_{1}\approx\hat{T}(Z^{k}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Main Results and Properties</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide more in-depth discussions
on the main properties of rectified flow.
We keep the discussion informal
to highlight the intuitions in this section and defer the
full course theoretical analysis to Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Flow Straight and Fast: Learning to Generate and Transfer Data with Rectified Flow" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">First,
for a given input coupling <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{0},X_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{0},X_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>,
it is easy to see that the exact minimum of (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In Rectified flow ‣ 2.1 Overview ‣ 2 Method ‣ Flow Straight and Fast: Learning to Generate and Transfer Data with Rectified Flow" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) is achieved if</p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle v^{X}(x,t)=\mathbb{E}[X_{1}-X_{0}\penalty\ |\penalty\ X_{t}=x]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>X</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.667em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle v^{X}(x,t)=\mathbb{E}[X_{1}-X_{0}\penalty\ |\penalty\ X_{t}=x],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">which is the expectation of the line directions <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}-X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}-X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> that pass through <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> at time <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.
We discuss below the property of rectified flow <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{d}Z_{t}=v^{X}(Z_{t},t)\mathrm{d}t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>X</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{d}Z_{t}=v^{X}(Z_{t},t)\mathrm{d}t</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{0}\sim{\pi}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{0}\sim{\pi}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>,
assuming that the ODE has an unique solution.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Marginal preserving property</h5>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">[Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 3.3. ‣ 3.1 The Marginal Preserving Property ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Flow Straight and Fast: Learning to Generate and Transfer Data with Rectified Flow" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>] <em id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">The pair <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Z_{0},Z_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Z_{0},Z_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> is a coupling of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In fact, the marginal law of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> equals that of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> at every time <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, that is, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Law}(Z_{t})=\mathrm{Law}(X_{t}),\forall t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Law</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Law</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Law}(Z_{t})=\mathrm{Law}(X_{t}),\forall t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. </em></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Intuitively, this is because, by the definition of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>X</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{X}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2.2 Main Results and Properties ‣ 2 Method ‣ Flow Straight and Fast: Learning to Generate and Transfer Data with Rectified Flow" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), the expected amount of mass
that passes through every infinitesmal volume at all location and time are equal under the dynamics of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, which ensures that they trace out the same marginal distributions:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\text{
Flow in $\&amp;$ out}\!\left(\vbox{\hbox{\includegraphics[height=25.83325pt]{arxiv_figures/arrow11_new.png}}}\right)\!\!=\text{Flow in $\&amp;$ out}\!\left(\vbox{\hbox{\includegraphics[height=25.83325pt]{arxiv_figures/arrow22_new.png}}}\right)\!,\penalty\ \forall\text{time $\&amp;$ location}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext> Flow in </mtext><mo>&amp;</mo><mtext> out</mtext></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtext class="ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:36/36"><img src="2209.03003v1/arxiv_figures/arrow11_new.png" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:36/36;" width="36" height="36" alt="[Uncaptioned image]"></mtext><mpadded style="width:0.327em;" width="0.327em"><mo>)</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Flow in </mtext><mo>&amp;</mo><mtext> out</mtext></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtext class="ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:33/36"><img src="2209.03003v1/arxiv_figures/arrow22_new.png" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:33/36;" width="33" height="36" alt="[Uncaptioned image]"></mtext><mpadded style="width:0.219em;" width="0.219em"><mo>)</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mrow><mtext>time </mtext><mo>&amp;</mo><mtext> location</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{
Flow in $\&amp;$ out}\!\left(\vbox{\hbox{\includegraphics[height=25.83325pt]{arxiv_figures/arrow11_new.png}}}\right)\!\!=\text{Flow in $\&amp;$ out}\!\left(\vbox{\hbox{\includegraphics[height=25.83325pt]{arxiv_figures/arrow22_new.png}}}\right)\!,\penalty\ \forall\text{time $\&amp;$ location}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\implies" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\implies</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{Law}(Z_{t})=\mathrm{Law}(X_{t}),\forall t." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Law</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Law</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{Law}(Z_{t})=\mathrm{Law}(X_{t}),\forall t.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">On the other hand, the joint distributions of the whole trajectory of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and that of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> are different in general.
In particular, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is in general a non-causal, non-Markov process,
with <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{0},X_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{0},X_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> a stochastic coupling,
and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">causalizes</em>, <em id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Markovianizes</em>
and <em id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.2.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">derandomizes</em> <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, while preserving the marginal distributions at all time.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reducing transport costs</h5>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">[Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 3.5. ‣ 3.2 Reducing Convex Transport Costs ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Flow Straight and Fast: Learning to Generate and Transfer Data with Rectified Flow" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>] <em id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">The coupling <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Z_{0},Z_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Z_{0},Z_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> yields lower or equal convex transport costs than the input <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{0},X_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{0},X_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> in that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[c(Z_{1}-Z_{0})]\leq\mathbb{E}[c(X_{1}-X_{0})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[c(Z_{1}-Z_{0})]\leq\mathbb{E}[c(X_{1}-X_{0})]</annotation></semantics></math> for any convex cost <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\colon\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\colon\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. </em></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The transport costs measure the expense of transporting the mass of one distribution to another following the assignment relation specified by the coupling and is a central topic in optimal transport <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">84</a>, <a href="#bib.bib85" title="" class="ltx_ref">85</a>, <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>, <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, e.g.,]</cite>. Typical examples are <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(\cdot)=\left\lVert\cdot\right\rVert^{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi>α</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(\cdot)=\left\lVert\cdot\right\rVert^{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{Rectify}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚁𝚎𝚌𝚝𝚒𝚏𝚢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{Rectify}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>
yields a Pareto descent on the collection of all convex transport costs, without targeting any specific <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>.
This distinguishes it from the
typical optimal transport optimization methods, which are explicitly framed
to optimize a given <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>.
As a result, recursive application of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{Rectify}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚁𝚎𝚌𝚝𝚒𝚏𝚢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{Rectify}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>
does not guarantee to attain the <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>-optimal coupling for any given <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, with the exception
in the one-dimensional case when the fixed point of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{Rectify}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚁𝚎𝚌𝚝𝚒𝚏𝚢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{Rectify}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> coincides with the unique monotonic coupling that simultaneously minimizes all non-negative convex costs <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>; see Section <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 Straight vs. Optimal Couplings ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Flow Straight and Fast: Learning to Generate and Transfer Data with Rectified Flow" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Intuitively,
the convex transport costs
are guaranteed to decrease because the paths of the rectified flow <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is a rewiring of the straight paths connecting <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{0},X_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{0},X_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>.
To give an illustration, consider the simple case of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(\cdot)=\left\lVert\cdot\right\rVert" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>‖</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(\cdot)=\left\lVert\cdot\right\rVert</annotation></semantics></math> when transport costs <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\left\lVert X_{0}-X_{1}\right\rVert]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\left\lVert X_{0}-X_{1}\right\rVert]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\left\lVert Z_{0}-Z_{1}\right\rVert]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\left\lVert Z_{0}-Z_{1}\right\rVert]</annotation></semantics></math>
are the expected length of the straight lines connecting the end points.
The inequality can be proved graphically as follows:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{
$\mathbb{E}[\left\lVert Z_{0}-Z_{1}\right\rVert]$
$=$
Length$\left(\vbox{\hbox{\includegraphics[height=25.83325pt]{arxiv_figures/=.png}}}\right)$
$\overset{\scriptsize(*)}{\leq}$
Length$\left(\vbox{\hbox{\includegraphics[height=25.83325pt]{arxiv_figures/v2.png}}}\right)$ $\overset{\scriptsize(**)}{=}$ Length$\left(\vbox{\hbox{\includegraphics[height=25.83325pt]{arxiv_figures/x.png}}}\right)$
$=$ ${\mathbb{E}[\left\lVert X_{0}-X_{1}\right\rVert]}$
}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mtext> </mtext><mo>=</mo><mtext> Length</mtext><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtext class="ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:39/36"><img src="2209.03003v1/arxiv_figures/=.png" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:39/36;" width="39" height="36" alt="[Uncaptioned image]"></mtext><mo>)</mo></mrow><mtext> </mtext><mover accent="true"><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mover><mtext> Length</mtext><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtext class="ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:39/36"><img src="2209.03003v1/arxiv_figures/v2.png" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:39/36;" width="39" height="36" alt="[Uncaptioned image]"></mtext><mo>)</mo></mrow><mtext> </mtext><mover accent="true"><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mover><mtext> Length</mtext><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtext class="ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:39/36"><img src="2209.03003v1/arxiv_figures/x.png" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:39/36;" width="39" height="36" alt="[Uncaptioned image]"></mtext><mo>)</mo></mrow><mtext> </mtext><mo>=</mo><mtext> </mtext><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{
$\mathbb{E}[\left\lVert Z_{0}-Z_{1}\right\rVert]$
$=$
Length$\left(\vbox{\hbox{\includegraphics[height=25.83325pt]{arxiv_figures/=.png}}}\right)$
$\overset{\scriptsize(*)}{\leq}$
Length$\left(\vbox{\hbox{\includegraphics[height=25.83325pt]{arxiv_figures/v2.png}}}\right)$ $\overset{\scriptsize(**)}{=}$ Length$\left(\vbox{\hbox{\includegraphics[height=25.83325pt]{arxiv_figures/x.png}}}\right)$
$=$ ${\mathbb{E}[\left\lVert X_{0}-X_{1}\right\rVert]}$
},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overset{\scriptsize(*)}{\leq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overset{\scriptsize(*)}{\leq}</annotation></semantics></math> uses
the triangle inequality,
and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overset{\scriptsize(**)}{=}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overset{\scriptsize(**)}{=}</annotation></semantics></math> holds because the paths of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is a rewiring
of the straight paths of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, following the
construction of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{{\boldsymbol{X}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>𝑿</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{{\boldsymbol{X}}}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2.2 Main Results and Properties ‣ 2 Method ‣ Flow Straight and Fast: Learning to Generate and Transfer Data with Rectified Flow" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).
For general convex <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>,
a similar proof using Jensen’s inequality is shown in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Reducing Convex Transport Costs ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Flow Straight and Fast: Learning to Generate and Transfer Data with Rectified Flow" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reflow, straightening, fast simulation</h5>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Figure <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Reflow, straightening, fast simulation ‣ 2.2 Main Results and Properties ‣ 2 Method ‣ Flow Straight and Fast: Learning to Generate and Transfer Data with Rectified Flow" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>,
when we recursively apply the procedure <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\boldsymbol{Z}}^{k+1}=\mathtt{RectFlow}((Z_{0}^{k},Z_{1}^{k}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝒁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝚁𝚎𝚌𝚝𝙵𝚕𝚘𝚠</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\boldsymbol{Z}}^{k+1}=\mathtt{RectFlow}((Z_{0}^{k},Z_{1}^{k}))</annotation></semantics></math>,
the paths of the <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-rectified flow <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\boldsymbol{Z}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒁</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\boldsymbol{Z}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>
are increasingly
straight, and hence easier to simulate numerically, as <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> increases. This straightening tendency can be guaranteed theoretically.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<p id="S2.F3.2" class="ltx_p">

<span id="S2.F3.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span class="ltx_tbody">
<span id="S2.F3.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S2.F3.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">
<span id="S2.F3.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top"><img src="2209.03003v1/arxiv_figures/circle06h0p1_v2_0.png" id="S2.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:130/86;" width="130" height="86" alt="Refer to caption">
</span></span>
<span id="S2.F3.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">
<span id="S2.F3.2.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top"><img src="2209.03003v1/arxiv_figures/circle06h0p1_v2_1.png" id="S2.F3.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:130/86;" width="130" height="86" alt="Refer to caption">
</span></span>
