<span id="S2.F3.2.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top"><img src="2209.03003v1/arxiv_figures/circle06h0p1_v2_2.png" id="S2.F3.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:130/86;" width="130" height="86" alt="Refer to caption">
</span></span>
<span id="S2.F3.2.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">
<span id="S2.F3.2.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top"><img src="2209.03003v1/arxiv_figures/circle06h0p1_v2_loss.png" id="S2.F3.g4" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:83/86;" width="83" height="86" alt="Refer to caption">
</span></span></span>
<span id="S2.F3.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S2.F3.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">
<span id="S2.F3.2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="S2.F3.2.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S2.F3.2.1.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span id="S2.F3.2.1.2.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)The 1st rectified flow <math id="S2.F3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\boldsymbol{Z}}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒁</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\boldsymbol{Z}}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></span>
<span id="S2.F3.2.1.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S2.F3.2.1.2.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><math id="S2.F3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\boldsymbol{Z}}^{1}=\mathtt{RectFlow}((X_{0},X_{1}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝒁</mi><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></msup><mo mathsize="0.700em">=</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝚁𝚎𝚌𝚝𝙵𝚕𝚘𝚠</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">X</mi><mn mathsize="0.700em">0</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">X</mi><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\boldsymbol{Z}}^{1}=\mathtt{RectFlow}((X_{0},X_{1}))</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
</span></span>
<span id="S2.F3.2.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">
<span id="S2.F3.2.1.2.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="S2.F3.2.1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S2.F3.2.1.2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span id="S2.F3.2.1.2.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b) Reflow <math id="S2.F3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\boldsymbol{Z}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒁</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\boldsymbol{Z}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></span>
<span id="S2.F3.2.1.2.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S2.F3.2.1.2.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><math id="S2.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\boldsymbol{Z}}^{2}=\mathtt{RectFlow}((Z_{0}^{1},Z_{1}^{1}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝒁</mi><mn mathsize="0.700em">2</mn></msup><mo mathsize="0.700em">=</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝚁𝚎𝚌𝚝𝙵𝚕𝚘𝚠</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.700em">Z</mi><mn mathsize="0.700em">0</mn><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></msubsup><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.700em">Z</mi><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></msubsup><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\boldsymbol{Z}}^{2}=\mathtt{RectFlow}((Z_{0}^{1},Z_{1}^{1}))</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
</span></span>
<span id="S2.F3.2.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">
<span id="S2.F3.2.1.2.3.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="S2.F3.2.1.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S2.F3.2.1.2.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span id="S2.F3.2.1.2.3.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c) Reflow <math id="S2.F3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\boldsymbol{Z}}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒁</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\boldsymbol{Z}}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></span>
<span id="S2.F3.2.1.2.3.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S2.F3.2.1.2.3.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><math id="S2.F3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\boldsymbol{Z}}^{3}=\mathtt{RectFlow}((Z_{0}^{2},Z_{1}^{2}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝒁</mi><mn mathsize="0.700em">3</mn></msup><mo mathsize="0.700em">=</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝚁𝚎𝚌𝚝𝙵𝚕𝚘𝚠</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.700em">Z</mi><mn mathsize="0.700em">0</mn><mn mathsize="0.700em">2</mn></msubsup><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.700em">Z</mi><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn><mn mathsize="0.700em">2</mn></msubsup><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\boldsymbol{Z}}^{3}=\mathtt{RectFlow}((Z_{0}^{2},Z_{1}^{2}))</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
</span></span>
<span id="S2.F3.2.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">
<span id="S2.F3.2.1.2.4.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="S2.F3.2.1.2.4.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S2.F3.2.1.2.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span id="S2.F3.2.1.2.4.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(d)
Transport cost,</span></span></span>
<span id="S2.F3.2.1.2.4.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S2.F3.2.1.2.4.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span id="S2.F3.2.1.2.4.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Straightness</span></span></span>
</span></span></span>
</span>
</span></p>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>(a)-(c) Samples of trajectories drawn from the reflows on a toy example (<math id="S2.F3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">π</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.F3.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">: purple dots</span>,
<math id="S2.F3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">π</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.F3.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">: red dots</span>;
the green and blue lines are trajectories connecting different modes of <math id="S2.F3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi}_{0},{\pi}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi}_{0},{\pi}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>).
(d) The
straightness and the relative L2 transport cost v.s.
the reflow steps; the values are scaled into <math id="S2.F3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, so 0 corresponds to straight lines and <math id="S2.F3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> optimal transport; see Section <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 Toy Examples ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Flow Straight and Fast: Learning to Generate and Transfer Data with Rectified Flow" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a> for more information. We use the non-parametric model in (<a href="#S2.E5" title="In Smooth function approximation ‣ 2.2 Main Results and Properties ‣ 2 Method ‣ Flow Straight and Fast: Learning to Generate and Transfer Data with Rectified Flow" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) with bandwidth <math id="S2.F3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically,
we say that a flow <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{d}Z_{t}=v(Z_{t},t)\mathrm{d}t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{d}Z_{t}=v(Z_{t},t)\mathrm{d}t</annotation></semantics></math> is straight if we have almost surely that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{t}=tZ_{1}+(1-t)Z_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{t}=tZ_{1}+(1-t)Z_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>,
or equivalently <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v(Z_{t},t)=Z_{1}-Z_{0}=const" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v(Z_{t},t)=Z_{1}-Z_{0}=const</annotation></semantics></math> following each path.
(More precisely, “straight” here refers to straight with a constant speed.)
Such straight flows are highly attractive computationally
as it is effective a one-step model:
a single Euler step update <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{1}=Z_{0}+v(Z_{0},0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{1}=Z_{0}+v(Z_{0},0)</annotation></semantics></math> calculates the exact <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that the linear interpolation <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\boldsymbol{X}}=\{X_{t}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\boldsymbol{X}}=\{X_{t}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is straight by this definition but it is not a (causal) flow and hence can not be simulated without an oracle assess to draws of both <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In comparison, it is non-trivial
to make a flow <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{d}Z_{t}=v(Z_{t},t)\mathrm{d}t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{d}Z_{t}=v(Z_{t},t)\mathrm{d}t</annotation></semantics></math> straight, because if so <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> must satisfy the inviscid Burgers’ equation <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{t}v+(\partial_{z}v)v=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>z</mi></msub><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{t}v+(\partial_{z}v)v=0</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}t}v(Z_{t},t)=\partial_{z}v(Z_{t},t)\dot{Z}_{t}+\partial_{t}v(Z_{t},t)=\partial_{z}v(Z_{t},t)v(Z_{t},t)+\partial_{t}v(Z_{t},t)=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>z</mi></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>z</mi></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}t}v(Z_{t},t)=\partial_{z}v(Z_{t},t)\dot{Z}_{t}+\partial_{t}v(Z_{t},t)=\partial_{z}v(Z_{t},t)v(Z_{t},t)+\partial_{t}v(Z_{t},t)=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">More generally,
we can measure the straightness of any
continuously differentiable process <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\boldsymbol{Z}}=\{Z_{t}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒁</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\boldsymbol{Z}}=\{Z_{t}\}</annotation></semantics></math> by</p>
<table id="S2.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle S({\boldsymbol{Z}})=\int_{0}^{1}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\lVert(Z_{1}-Z_{0})-\dot{Z}_{t}\right\rVert^{2}\right]\mathrm{d}t." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒁</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle S({\boldsymbol{Z}})=\int_{0}^{1}\mathbb{E}\left[\left\lVert(Z_{1}-Z_{0})-\dot{Z}_{t}\right\rVert^{2}\right]\mathrm{d}t.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S({\boldsymbol{Z}})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒁</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S({\boldsymbol{Z}})=0</annotation></semantics></math> means exact straightness.
A flow whose <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S({\boldsymbol{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒁</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S({\boldsymbol{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> is small
has nearly straight paths and hence can be simulated accurately using numerical solvers with a small number of discretization steps. Section <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 The Straightening Effect ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Flow Straight and Fast: Learning to Generate and Transfer Data with Rectified Flow" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> shows that
applying rectification recursively
provably decreases
<math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S({\boldsymbol{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒁</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S({\boldsymbol{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> towards zero.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">[Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm7" title="Theorem 3.7. ‣ 𝑂(1/𝐾) convergence rate ‣ 3.3 The Straightening Effect ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Flow Straight and Fast: Learning to Generate and Transfer Data with Rectified Flow" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.7</span></a>]
Let <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\boldsymbol{Z}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐙</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\boldsymbol{Z}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th rectified flow induced from <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{0},X_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{0},X_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then</em></p>
<table id="S2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\min_{k\in\{0\cdots K\}}S({\boldsymbol{Z}}^{k})\leq\frac{\mathbb{E}[\left\lVert X_{1}-X_{0}\right\rVert^{2}]}{K}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></munder><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒁</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>K</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{k\in\{0\cdots K\}}S({\boldsymbol{Z}}^{k})\leq\frac{\mathbb{E}[\left\lVert X_{1}-X_{0}\right\rVert^{2}]}{K}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">As shown Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Flow Straight and Fast: Learning to Generate and Transfer Data with Rectified Flow" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>,
applying one step of reflow can already provide nearly straight flows that yield good performance when simulated with a single Euler step.
It is not recommended to apply
too many reflow steps as it may accumulate estimation error on <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>X</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Distillation</h5>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After obtaining the <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th rectified flow <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\boldsymbol{Z}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒁</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\boldsymbol{Z}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can further improve the inference speed by distilling the relation of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Z_{0}^{k},Z_{1}^{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Z_{0}^{k},Z_{1}^{k})</annotation></semantics></math> into a neural network <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{T}</annotation></semantics></math> to directly predict <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{1}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{1}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{0}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{0}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> without simulating the flow.
Given that the flow is already nearly straight (and hence well approximated by the one-step update),
and the distillation can be done efficiently.
In particular, if we take <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{T}(z_{0})=z_{0}+v(z_{0},0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{T}(z_{0})=z_{0}+v(z_{0},0)</annotation></semantics></math>,
then the loss function for distilling <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\boldsymbol{Z}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒁</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\boldsymbol{Z}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\left[\left\lVert(Z_{1}^{k}-Z_{0}^{k})-v(Z_{0}^{k},0)\right\rVert^{2}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\left[\left\lVert(Z_{1}^{k}-Z_{0}^{k})-v(Z_{0}^{k},0)\right\rVert^{2}\right]</annotation></semantics></math>, which is the term in (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In Rectified flow ‣ 2.1 Overview ‣ 2 Method ‣ Flow Straight and Fast: Learning to Generate and Transfer Data with Rectified Flow" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) when <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We should highlight the difference between distillation and rectification: distillation attempts to faithfully approximate the coupling <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Z_{0}^{k},Z_{1}^{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Z_{0}^{k},Z_{1}^{k})</annotation></semantics></math>
while rectification yields a different coupling <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Z_{0}^{k+1},Z_{1}^{k+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Z_{0}^{k+1},Z_{1}^{k+1})</annotation></semantics></math>
with lower transport cost and more straight flow.
Hence, distillation should be applied only in the final stage when we want to fine-tune the model for fast one-step inference.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">On the velocity field <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>X</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{X}</annotation></semantics></math></h5>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> yields a conditional density function <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(x_{0}\penalty\ |\penalty\ x_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.500em">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(x_{0}\penalty\ |\penalty\ x_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> when conditioned on <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}=x_{1}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}=x_{1},</annotation></semantics></math> then the optimal velocity field <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{X}(z,t)=\mathbb{E}[X_{1}-X_{0}|X_{t}=z]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>X</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{X}(z,t)=\mathbb{E}[X_{1}-X_{0}|X_{t}=z]</annotation></semantics></math> can be represented by</p>
<table id="S2.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle v^{X}(z,t)=\mathbb{E}\left[\frac{X_{1}-z}{1-t}\eta_{t}(X_{1},z)\right]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>X</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle v^{X}(z,t)=\mathbb{E}\left[\frac{X_{1}-z}{1-t}\eta_{t}(X_{1},z)\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\eta_{t}(X_{1},z)={\rho\left(\frac{z-tX_{1}}{1-t}\penalty\ \bigg|\penalty\ X_{1}\right)}\bigg/{\mathbb{E}\left[\rho\left(\frac{z-tX_{1}}{1-t}\penalty\ \bigg|\penalty\ X_{1}\right)\right]}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.500em" mathsize="2.100em" rspace="0.500em">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.500em" mathsize="2.100em" rspace="0.500em">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\eta_{t}(X_{1},z)={\rho\left(\frac{z-tX_{1}}{1-t}\penalty\ \bigg|\penalty\ X_{1}\right)}\bigg/{\mathbb{E}\left[\rho\left(\frac{z-tX_{1}}{1-t}\penalty\ \bigg|\penalty\ X_{1}\right)\right]},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where the expectation <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\left[\cdot\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\left[\cdot\right]</annotation></semantics></math> is taken w.r.t. <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}\sim{\pi}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}\sim{\pi}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. This can be seen by noting that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}=\frac{z-tX_{1}}{1-t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}=\frac{z-tX_{1}}{1-t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}-X_{0}=\frac{X_{1}-z}{1-t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}-X_{0}=\frac{X_{1}-z}{1-t}</annotation></semantics></math>, when conditioned on <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}=z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}=z</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence, if <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is positive and continuous everywhere, then <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>X</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is well defined and continuous on <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}\times[0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}\times[0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Further, if <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log\eta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log\eta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is continuously differentiable w.r.t. <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>, we can show that</p>
<table id="S2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{z}v^{X}(z,t)=\frac{1}{1-t}\mathbb{E}\left[\left(({X_{1}-z})\nabla_{z}\log\eta_{t}(X_{1},z)-1\right)\eta_{t}(X_{1},z)\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>z</mi></msub><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>X</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{z}v^{X}(z,t)=\frac{1}{1-t}\mathbb{E}\left[\left(({X_{1}-z})\nabla_{z}\log\eta_{t}(X_{1},z)-1\right)\eta_{t}(X_{1},z)\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Note that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{d}Z_{t}=v^{X}(Z_{t},t)\mathrm{d}t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>X</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{d}Z_{t}=v^{X}(Z_{t},t)\mathrm{d}t</annotation></semantics></math>
is guaranteed to have a unique solution if
<math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>X</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is uniformly Lipschitz continuous on <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,a]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,a]</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}|X_{1}=x_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}|X_{1}=x_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> does not yield a conditional density function,
<math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{X}(z,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>X</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{X}(z,t)</annotation></semantics></math> may be undefined or discontinuous,
making the ODE <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{d}Z_{t}=v^{X}(Z_{t},t)\mathrm{d}t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>X</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{d}Z_{t}=v^{X}(Z_{t},t)\mathrm{d}t</annotation></semantics></math>
ill-behaved.
A simple fix is to add <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> with a Gaussian noise <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^{2}I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^{2}I)</annotation></semantics></math> independent of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{0},X_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{0},X_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> to yield a smoothed variable <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{X}_{0}=X_{0}+\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{X}_{0}=X_{0}+\xi</annotation></semantics></math>, and transfer <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{X}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{X}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> using rectified flow.
This would effectively give a randomized mapping of form <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(X_{0}+\xi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(X_{0}+\xi)</annotation></semantics></math> transporting <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px6" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Smooth function approximation</h5>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following (<a href="#S2.E4" title="In On the velocity field 𝑣^𝑋 ‣ 2.2 Main Results and Properties ‣ 2 Method ‣ Flow Straight and Fast: Learning to Generate and Transfer Data with Rectified Flow" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>),
we can <em id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">exactly</em> calculate <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>X</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{X}</annotation></semantics></math>
if the conditional density function <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\rho(\cdot|x_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(\cdot|x_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> exists and is known,
and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the empirical measure of a finite number of points (whose expectation can be evaluated exactly).
In this case, running the rectified flow
forwardly would
precisely recover the points in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This, however, is not practically useful in most cases as it completely overfits the data.
Hence, it is both necessary and beneficial
to fit
<math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>X</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{X}</annotation></semantics></math> with a smooth function approximator such as neural network or non-parametric models,
to obtain
smoothed distributions
with novel samples
that are practically useful.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Deep neural networks are no doubt the best function approximators for large scale problems.
For low dimensional problems,
the following simple Nadaraya–Watson style non-parametric estimator of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>X</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{X}</annotation></semantics></math>
can yield a good approximation to the exact rectified flow
without knowing
the conditional density <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle v^{X,h}(z,t)=\mathbb{E}\left[\frac{X_{1}-z}{1-t}\omega_{h}(X_{t},z)\right]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle v^{X,h}(z,t)=\mathbb{E}\left[\frac{X_{1}-z}{1-t}\omega_{h}(X_{t},z)\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{h}(X_{t},z)=\frac{\kappa_{h}(X_{t},z)}{\mathbb{E}\left[\kappa_{h}(X_{t},z)\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{h}(X_{t},z)=\frac{\kappa_{h}(X_{t},z)}{\mathbb{E}\left[\kappa_{h}(X_{t},z)\right]}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{h}(x,z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{h}(x,z)</annotation></semantics></math> is a smoothing kernel with a bandwith parameter <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>
that measures the similarity between <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.
Taking the Gaussian RBF kernel <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p2.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\kappa_{h}(x,z)=\exp(-\left\lVert x-z\right\rVert^{2}/2h^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{h}(x,z)=\exp(-\left\lVert x-z\right\rVert^{2}/2h^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>,
then when <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\to 0^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>0</mn><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\to 0^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>,
it can be shown that
<math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{X,h}(z,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{X,h}(z,t)</annotation></semantics></math> converges to <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{X}(z,t)=\mathbb{E}\left[\frac{X_{1}-z}{1-t}\penalty\ |\penalty\ X_{t}=z\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>X</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo fence="false" lspace="0.500em" rspace="0.667em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{X}(z,t)=\mathbb{E}\left[\frac{X_{1}-z}{1-t}\penalty\ |\penalty\ X_{t}=z\right]</annotation></semantics></math>
on points <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>
that can be attained by <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., the conditional expectation <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p2.m14" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}\left[\cdot\penalty\ |\penalty\ X_{t}=z\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.500em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.667em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\left[\cdot\penalty\ |\penalty\ X_{t}=z\right]</annotation></semantics></math> exists. ).
On points <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> can not attain,
<math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{X,h}(z,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{X,h}(z,t)</annotation></semantics></math> extrapolates the value by finding the <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> that is close to <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>.
In practice, we replace the expectations in (<a href="#S2.E5" title="In Smooth function approximation ‣ 2.2 Main Results and Properties ‣ 2 Method ‣ Flow Straight and Fast: Learning to Generate and Transfer Data with Rectified Flow" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) with empirical averaging.
We find that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{X,h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{X,h}</annotation></semantics></math> performs well in practice
because it is a mixture of linear functions that always point to a point
in the support of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>A Nonlinear Extension</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present a nonlinear
extension of rectified flow
in which the linear interpolation <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is replaced by any time-differentiable curve connecting <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Such generalized rectified flows can still transport <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> (Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 3.3. ‣ 3.1 The Marginal Preserving Property ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Flow Straight and Fast: Learning to Generate and Transfer Data with Rectified Flow" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>),
but no longer guarantee to decrease convex transport costs, or have the straightening effect.
Importantly,
the method of probability flows <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>]</cite> and DDIM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>]</cite> can be viewed (approximately) as special cases of this framework,
allows us to clarify the connection with and the advantages over these methods.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let
<math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\boldsymbol{X}}}=\{X_{t}\colon t\in[0,1]\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\boldsymbol{X}}}=\{X_{t}\colon t\in[0,1]\}</annotation></semantics></math> be any time-differentiable
random process that connects <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{X}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{X}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> be the time derivative of <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The (nonlinear) rectified flow induced from <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\boldsymbol{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑿</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\boldsymbol{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</p>
<table id="S2.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{d}Z_{t}=v^{{\boldsymbol{X}}}(Z_{t},t)\mathrm{d}t,\penalty\ \penalty\ \penalty\ \text{with}\penalty\ \penalty\ \penalty\ \penalty\ Z_{0}=X_{0},\penalty\ \penalty\ \penalty\ \penalty\ \text{and}\penalty\ \penalty\ \penalty\ \penalty\ v^{{\boldsymbol{X}}}(z,t)=\mathbb{E}\left[\dot{X}_{t}\penalty\ |\penalty\ X_{t}=t\right]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>𝑿</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.667em">,</mo><mtext>with</mtext><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>𝑿</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.667em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{d}Z_{t}=v^{{\boldsymbol{X}}}(Z_{t},t)\mathrm{d}t,\penalty\ \penalty\ \penalty\ \text{with}\penalty\ \penalty\ \penalty\ \penalty\ Z_{0}=X_{0},\penalty\ \penalty\ \penalty\ \penalty\ \text{and}\penalty\ \penalty\ \penalty\ \penalty\ v^{{\boldsymbol{X}}}(z,t)=\mathbb{E}\left[\dot{X}_{t}\penalty\ |\penalty\ X_{t}=t\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">We can estimate <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{{\boldsymbol{X}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>𝑿</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{{\boldsymbol{X}}}</annotation></semantics></math> by solving</p>
<table id="S2.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{v}\int_{0}^{1}\mathbb{E}\left[w_{t}\left\lVert v(X_{t},\penalty\ t)-\dot{X}_{t}\right\rVert^{2}\right]\mathrm{d}t," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mmultiscripts><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>v</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mstyle><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{v}\int_{0}^{1}\mathbb{E}\left[w_{t}\left\lVert v(X_{t},\penalty\ t)-\dot{X}_{t}\right\rVert^{2}\right]\mathrm{d}t,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{t}\colon(0,1)\to(0,+\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{t}\colon(0,1)\to(0,+\infty)</annotation></semantics></math> is a positive weighting sequence (<math id="S2.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{t}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{t}=1</annotation></semantics></math> by default).
When using the linear interpolation <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}=tX_{1}+(1-t)X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}=tX_{1}+(1-t)X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{X}_{t}=X_{1}-X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{X}_{t}=X_{1}-X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and
(<a href="#S2.E6" title="In 2.3 A Nonlinear Extension ‣ 2 Method ‣ Flow Straight and Fast: Learning to Generate and Transfer Data with Rectified Flow" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) with <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{t}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{t}=1</annotation></semantics></math> reduces to (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In Rectified flow ‣ 2.1 Overview ‣ 2 Method ‣ Flow Straight and Fast: Learning to Generate and Transfer Data with Rectified Flow" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).
As we show in Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 3.3. ‣ 3.1 The Marginal Preserving Property ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Flow Straight and Fast: Learning to Generate and Transfer Data with Rectified Flow" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>,
the flow <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\boldsymbol{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒁</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\boldsymbol{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> given by this method
still preserves the marginal laws of <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\boldsymbol{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑿</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\boldsymbol{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>, that is, <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Law}(Z_{t})=\mathrm{Law}(X_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Law</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Law</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Law}(Z_{t})=\mathrm{Law}(X_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, and hence
<math id="S2.SS3.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Z_{0},Z_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Z_{0},Z_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> remains to be a coupling of <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi}_{0},{\pi}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi}_{0},{\pi}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
However, if <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\boldsymbol{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑿</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\boldsymbol{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is not straight,
<math id="S2.SS3.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Z_{0},Z_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Z_{0},Z_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> no longer guarantees to decrease the convex transport costs over <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{0},X_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{0},X_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>. More importantly, the reflow procedure no longer straightens the paths of <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A simple class of interpolation processes is <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}=\alpha_{t}X_{1}+\beta_{t}X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}=\alpha_{t}X_{1}+\beta_{t}X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>
are two differentiable sequences that satisfy <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{1}=\beta_{0}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{1}=\beta_{0}=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{0}=\beta_{1}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{0}=\beta_{1}=0</annotation></semantics></math> to ensure that the process equals <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0},X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0},X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> at the starting and end points. In this case,
we have <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{X}_{t}=\dot{\alpha}_{t}X_{1}+\dot{\beta}_{t}X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>β</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{X}_{t}=\dot{\alpha}_{t}X_{1}+\dot{\beta}_{t}X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S2.E6" title="In 2.3 A Nonlinear Extension ‣ 2 Method ‣ Flow Straight and Fast: Learning to Generate and Transfer Data with Rectified Flow" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) where <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{\alpha}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{\alpha}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{\beta}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>β</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{\beta}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> are the time derivatives of <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The shape of the curve is controlled by the relation of <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. If we take <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{t}=1-\alpha_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{t}=1-\alpha_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> have straight paths but does not travel at a constant speed; it can be viewed as a time-changed variant of the canonical case <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}=tX_{1}+(1-t)X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}=tX_{1}+(1-t)X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is reparameterized to <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{t}\neq 1-\alpha_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{t}\neq 1-\alpha_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, the paths of <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>
are not straight lines except some special cases (e.g., <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{\alpha}_{t}X_{1}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{\alpha}_{t}X_{1}=0</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{\beta}_{t}X_{0}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>β</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{\beta}_{t}X_{0}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, or <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}=aX_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}=aX_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.3.1 </span>Probability Flow ODEs and DDIM</h4>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The probability flow ODEs (PF-ODEs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>]</cite> and denoising diffusion implicit models (DDIM) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>]</cite>
are methods for learning ODE-based generative models of <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> from a spherical Gaussian initial distribution <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, derived by converting a SDE learned by
denoising diffusion methods to an ODE
with equivalent marginal laws.
In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>]</cite>,
three types of PF-ODEs are derived from
three types of SDEs learned as score-based generative models, including
variance-exploding (VE) SDE,
variance-preserving (VP) SDE,
and sub-VP SDE,
which we denote by
VE ODE, VP ODE, and sub-VP ODE, respectively.
VP ODE is equivalent to the continuous time limit of DDIM,
which is derived from the denoising diffusion probability model (DDPM) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>.
As the derivations of PF-ODEs and DDIM
require advanced tools in stochastic calculus,
we limit our discussion on the final algorithmic procedures suggested in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, which we summarize in Section <a href="#S3.SS5" title="3.5 Denoising Diffusion Models and Probability Flow ODEs ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Flow Straight and Fast: Learning to Generate and Transfer Data with Rectified Flow" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>.
The readers are referred to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>, <a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>]</cite> for the details.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">[Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmthm11" title="Proposition 3.11. ‣ 3.5 Denoising Diffusion Models and Probability Flow ODEs ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Flow Straight and Fast: Learning to Generate and Transfer Data with Rectified Flow" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.11</span></a>]
All variants of PF-ODEs
can be
viewed as instances of (<a href="#S2.E6" title="In 2.3 A Nonlinear Extension ‣ 2 Method ‣ Flow Straight and Fast: Learning to Generate and Transfer Data with Rectified Flow" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>)
when using
<math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}=\alpha_{t}X_{1}+\beta_{t}\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}=\alpha_{t}X_{1}+\beta_{t}\xi</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t},\beta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t},\beta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{1}=1,\beta_{1}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{1}=1,\beta_{1}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, where
<math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)</annotation></semantics></math> is a standard Gaussian random variable.
</em></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Here we need to use introduce <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math> to replace <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> because the choices of <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> suggested in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>, <a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>
do not satisfy the boundary condition of <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{0}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{0}=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{0}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{0}=1</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and hence <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}\neq\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}\neq\xi</annotation></semantics></math>.
Instead,
in these methods, the initial distribution <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}\sim{\pi}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}\sim{\pi}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is implicitly defined as <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}=\alpha_{0}X_{1}+\beta_{0}\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}=\alpha_{0}X_{1}+\beta_{0}\xi</annotation></semantics></math>,
which is approximated by <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}\approx\beta_{0}\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}\approx\beta_{0}\xi</annotation></semantics></math> by making <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{0}X_{1}\ll\beta_{0}\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>≪</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{0}X_{1}\ll\beta_{0}\xi</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence, <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is set to be <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(0,\beta_{0}^{2}I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(0,\beta_{0}^{2}I)</annotation></semantics></math> in these methods.
Viewed through our framework,
there is no reason to restrict <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math> to be <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(0,\beta_{0}^{2}I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(0,\beta_{0}^{2}I)</annotation></semantics></math>, or not set <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{0}=0,\beta_{0}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{0}=0,\beta_{0}=1</annotation></semantics></math> to avoid the approximation.</p>
</div>
