<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>
Comparing rectified flow with VP ODE and sub-VP ODE when <math id="S2.F4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi}_{0}=\mathcal{N}(0,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi}_{0}=\mathcal{N}(0,I)</annotation></semantics></math> (purple dots) and <math id="S2.F4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is a low variance Gaussian mixture shown as the red dots.
The linear rectified flow yields nearly straight trajectories with one step of reflow. But the trajectories of VP ODE and sub-VP ODE are curved and can not be straightened by reflowing.
</figcaption>
</figure>
